  "ls.capture.title": "Take free pieces",
  "ls.capture.sub": "Never miss an undefended piece",
  "ls.capture.intro": "<p>If an enemy piece is <b>not defended</b>, you can take it without being recaptured. A <b>more valuable</b> piece is worth taking even when it is defended.</p><p>Before every move, ask: \"Can I take something? Is it defended?\"</p><p class=\"note\">Values: pawn 1, knight 3, bishop 3, rook 5, queen 9.</p>",
  "ls.capture.goal": "Find the capture that wins material.",
  "ls.capture.why": "{m} wins material: either nothing can recapture, or the recapture still leaves you ahead.",
  "ls.capture.hint": "Red frames mark enemy pieces you can take without losing material.",
  "ls.escape.title": "Save the attacked piece",
  "ls.escape.sub": "Move it or protect it in time",
  "ls.escape.intro": "<p>When one of your pieces <b>can be taken for free</b>, or is <b>attacked by a cheaper piece</b>, make it safe first.</p><p>You can move it away, defend it, block the attack, or capture the attacker. When moving away, check that the new square is safe.</p><p class=\"note\">Make it a habit to ask what your opponent's last move threatened.</p>",
  "ls.escape.goal": "Rescue the threatened piece.",
  "ls.escape.why": "{m} saves the piece that was under attack.",
  "ls.escape.hint": "Red frames mark your pieces that would be lost.",
  "ls.fork.title": "Forks",
  "ls.fork.sub": "One piece, two targets",
  "ls.fork.intro": "<p>A <b>fork</b> attacks two or more pieces at once. Your opponent can only save one of them.</p><p>Knight forks are the most famous, but pawns, bishops and queens fork too. A fork that gives <b>check</b> is especially strong.</p><p class=\"note\">Look for enemy king, queen and rooks standing a knight's jump apart.</p>",
  "ls.fork.goal": "Attack two pieces at once.",
  "ls.fork.why": "{m} attacks two pieces at once; your opponent cannot save both.",
  "ls.fork.hint": "The piece in the gold frame can hit two targets at once.",
  "ls.pin.title": "Pins and skewers",
  "ls.pin.sub": "Attack pieces standing in a line",
  "ls.pin.intro": "<p>When two enemy pieces stand on the line of your bishop, rook or queen, the front piece is <b>pinned</b>: if the king is behind it, it cannot move at all.</p><p>If the more valuable piece is in front, it must move away and you <b>take the piece behind</b> — a skewer.</p><p class=\"note\">Enemy pieces on one file, rank or diagonal are a chance for a pin.</p>",
  "ls.pin.goal": "Attack the pieces in line.",
  "ls.pin.why": "{m} pins or skewers the enemy pieces standing in line.",
  "ls.pin.hint": "Move the piece in the gold frame onto the line where two enemy pieces stand.",
  "ls.mate1.title": "Mate in one",
  "ls.mate1.sub": "Checkmate patterns",
  "ls.mate1.intro": "<p>Checkmate means the king has <b>no escape square</b>, and the check <b>cannot be blocked or captured</b>.</p><p>Common patterns are the <b>back-rank mate</b> against a king shut in by its own pawns, and a queen right in front of the king, protected by another piece.</p><p class=\"note\">After giving check, test the king's escape squares one by one.</p>",
  "ls.mate1.goal": "Checkmate in one move.",
  "ls.mate1.why": "{m} is checkmate: the king cannot escape and nothing can block.",
  "ls.mate1.hint": "The red frame is the enemy king. Work out its escape squares first.",
  "ls.mate2.title": "Mate in two",
  "ls.mate2.sub": "Drive the king with checks",
  "ls.mate2.intro": "<p>Even without an immediate mate, a move that <b>limits the replies</b> — a check, or a move that takes away escape squares — can lead to mate on the next move.</p><p>Sometimes a <b>sacrifice</b> lures the king out. It looks like a loss, but mate wins the game.</p><p class=\"note\">Check that every reply allows a mate next move.</p>",
  "ls.mate2.goal": "Find the first move of a mate in two.",
  "ls.mate2.why": "After {m}, every reply allows mate on the next move.",
  "ls.mate2.hint": "The red frame is the enemy king. Start with checks or moves that take away its squares.",
  "ls.promote.title": "Promote a pawn",
  "ls.promote.sub": "Pawns rule the endgame",
  "ls.promote.intro": "<p>With few pieces left, <b>pushing a pawn to the last rank and making a queen</b> decides the game.</p><p>A <b>passed pawn</b> — no enemy pawn can stop it — should advance early. Escort it with the king, and count whether the enemy king can catch it.</p><p class=\"note\">Compare the pawn's distance to the last rank with the enemy king's distance to the pawn.</p>",
  "ls.promote.goal": "Find the move that gets the pawn through.",
  "ls.promote.why": "{m} is best: it clears the pawn's way to promotion.",
  "ls.promote.hint": "Move the piece in the gold frame. Count the squares to the last rank.",
  "pz.title": "Mate puzzles",
  "pz.desc": "\"Mate in N\" problems. The defender always plays the most stubborn reply. The first move is unique, and every puzzle is verified by the engine.",
